.Dd July 9, 2026
.Dt XTC_ENV 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm xtc_env_get ,
.Nm xtc_env_set ,
.Nm xtc_rand_u64 ,
.Nm xtc_rand_seed ,
.Nm xtc_strlcpy ,
.Nm xtc_strlcat
.Nd thread-safe environment access, per-thread PRNG, and bounded string copy
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In xtc.h
.Ft const char *
.Fn xtc_env_get "const char *name"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_env_set "const char *name" "const char *value" "int overwrite"
.Ft uint64_t
.Fn xtc_rand_u64 "void"
.Ft void
.Fn xtc_rand_seed "uint64_t seed"
.Ft size_t
.Fn xtc_strlcpy "char *dst" "const char *src" "size_t dstsize"
.Ft size_t
.Fn xtc_strlcat "char *dst" "const char *src" "size_t dstsize"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
These entry points smooth three well-known threaded-C footguns in the
C library.
A CONSUMER of libxtc uses only these
.Fn xtc_* No functions and never the internal Fn __os_* No wrappers.
.Ss Thread-safe environment
.Xr getenv 3
and
.Xr setenv 3
are not safe against one another: a
.Xr setenv 3
from one thread can reallocate the
.Va environ
block that a concurrent
.Xr getenv 3
on another thread is reading.
.Fn xtc_env_get
and
.Fn xtc_env_set
serialize every access on a single process-wide lock.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_env_get
returns a pointer to the value of
.Fa name ,
or
.Dv NULL
if the variable is unset (or
.Fa name
is
.Dv NULL ) .
The value is copied into a small PER-THREAD buffer under the lock, so
the returned pointer cannot be invalidated by a
.Fn xtc_env_set
from another thread; it is valid until the next
.Fn xtc_env_get
call ON THE SAME THREAD.
Copy it if you need to keep it.
A value longer than the thread buffer is truncated but always
NUL-terminated.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_env_set
sets
.Fa name
to
.Fa value ;
when
.Fa overwrite
is 0 an existing variable is left unchanged.
.Ss Per-thread seedable PRNG
.Xr rand 3
and
.Xr random 3
share process-global state and are not thread-safe.
.Fn xtc_rand_u64
draws the next 64-bit value from a PER-THREAD
.Em splitmix64
stream, so every thread has its own state with no shared-state race
and no contention.
.Fn xtc_rand_seed
makes the calling thread's stream reproducible: seeding with the same
.Fa seed
yields the same sequence.
A thread that has not been seeded auto-seeds from the monotonic clock on
first draw, so distinct threads get distinct default streams.
This is NOT a cryptographic generator; do not use it for keys or
security tokens.
.Ss Bounded string copy
.Xr strncpy 3
does not NUL-terminate when the source is at least as long as the
buffer.
.Fn xtc_strlcpy
copies
.Fa src
into
.Fa dst
and
.Fn xtc_strlcat
appends
.Fa src
to the string already in
.Fa dst ,
both following the BSD (OpenBSD) contract: they ALWAYS NUL-terminate
when
.Fa dstsize
is greater than 0, and they return the total length they TRIED to
create.
A return value greater than or equal to
.Fa dstsize
means the result was truncated.
Both are pure and perform no allocation.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn xtc_env_get
returns a pointer to the variable's value (valid until the next
.Fn xtc_env_get
on the same thread), or
.Dv NULL
if the variable is unset.
.Fn xtc_env_set
returns
.Dv XTC_OK ,
or a negative
.Dv XTC_E_*
code:
.Dv XTC_E_INVAL
for a
.Dv NULL ,
empty, or
.Sq =
.No -containing Fa name ,
and
.Dv XTC_E_NOMEM
if the environment cannot grow.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_rand_u64
returns the next 64-bit pseudo-random value for the calling thread.
.Fn xtc_rand_seed
returns no value.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_strlcpy
returns
.Fn strlen Fa src ,
and
.Fn xtc_strlcat
returns the initial
.Fn strlen Fa dst
plus
.Fn strlen Fa src ;
a return value greater than or equal to
.Fa dstsize
indicates truncation.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr xtc_free 3 ,
.Xr getenv 3 ,
.Xr setenv 3 ,
.Xr strlcpy 3
